WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.700
In an adult human, a litre of arterial blood carries about 200 millilitres of

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.940
oxygen. If that blood were plasma alone, it would carry about three.

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.180
Oxygen dissolves poorly in water, and plasma is mostly water.

4
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.860
So how does blood make up the difference?

5
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:28.433
In mammals, almost all the oxygen rides on hemoglobin.

6
00:00:29.153 --> 00:00:33.473
That is an iron-containing protein packed inside red blood cells.

7
00:00:34.373 --> 00:00:39.953
Each human hemoglobin molecule has four chains and four oxygen-binding sites.

8
00:00:41.113 --> 00:00:44.613
Saturation is the fraction of those sites that hold oxygen.

9
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:51.197
Plot saturation against PO2, and you get the oxygen dissociation curve.

10
00:00:51.857 --> 00:00:54.157
For adult human blood it is S-shaped.

11
00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:59.977
Above about 60 millimetres of mercury, it is nearly flat, so blood in the lungs

12
00:00:59.977 --> 00:01:01.377
loads almost fully.

13
00:01:02.157 --> 00:01:08.857
Between about 10 and 40 it is steep, so a small fall in tissue PO2 releases a lot of

14
00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:12.457
oxygen. The shape comes from cooperative binding.

15
00:01:13.097 --> 00:01:17.477
Oxygen at one site makes the other sites on that molecule bind more readily.

16
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:21.437
Take an illustrative adult human at rest.

17
00:01:22.277 --> 00:01:28.077
Arterial PO2 is about 100 millimetres of mercury, at 98% saturation.

18
00:01:29.337 --> 00:01:33.197
Mixed venous blood is near 40 millimetres of mercury, at 75%.

19
00:01:33.937 --> 00:01:37.717
The tissues took 24% of the oxygen offered.

20
00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:44.737
With a cardiac output of 5 litres per minute, that is 240 millilitres of oxygen per

21
00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:48.697
minute. Three quarters of the oxygen comes back unused.

22
00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:51.737
That reserve sits on the steep part.

23
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:59.230
In Copenhagen in 1903, Christian Bohr and colleagues raised the carbon dioxide

24
00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:00.930
around fresh dog blood.

25
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:05.390
At the same oxygen pressure, it held less oxygen.

26
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:13.090
Today the Bohr effect names that fall in affinity when pH falls or carbon dioxide

27
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.630
rises. The curve shifts right, and P50 rises.

28
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.170
Higher temperature and more BPG also shift it right.

29
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.920
In respiring tissue, carbon dioxide is high and PO2 is low.

30
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.340
That is where the curve is steep.

31
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:38.300
At the same PO2, a right-shifted curve holds less oxygen, so the blood has released

32
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:45.360
more. In adult human blood, hemoglobin loads on the flat part and unloads on the steep part.
